   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "To endow every logic with a semantics of an algebraic kind one has to consider, at least, algebras together with a set of designated elements, without any requirement about its definability using the corresponding algebraic language. These pairs are the logical matrices. Tarski defined the general concept of logical matrix in the 1920s but the concept was already implicit in previous work by Łukasiewicz, Bernays, Post and others, who used truth-tables, either in independence proofs or to define ",
   "md_text": "To endow every logic with a semantics of an algebraic kind one has to consider, at least, algebras together with a set of designated elements, without any requirement about its definability using the corresponding algebraic language. These pairs are the logical matrices. Tarski defined the general concept of logical matrix in the 1920s but the concept was already implicit in previous work by Łukasiewicz, Bernays, Post and others, who used truth-tables, either in independence proofs or to define "
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Given a logical deduction system ⟦M⟧ and an arbitrary set of formulas ⟦M⟧ , a formula ⟦M⟧ is deducible in ⟦M⟧ from ⟦M⟧ if there is a finite sequence of formulas any one of which belongs to ⟦M⟧ or is an axiom of ⟦M⟧ or is obtained from previous formulas in the sequence by one of the inference rules of ⟦M⟧ . Such a sequence is a deduction (or proof) in ⟦M⟧ of ⟦M⟧ with premises or hypotheses in ⟦M⟧ . Let ⟦M⟧ be the set of formulas deducible in ⟦M⟧ from the formulas in ⟦M⟧ taken as premises or hypot",
